# Review reports, issue status and repair verification

Use for an artifact review report, a no-finding conclusion, issue storage or authorized fixes. A report summarizes actual reviewed evidence; neither six-principle counts nor a completed template certify overall acceptance.

## Scope, mode and coverage

State the actual object/version, goal, requested scope, review date, direct or genuinely independent mode, checks performed and inaccessible or unexamined material. Inventory untracked inputs when relevant. Include relevant call/data paths and source records instead of claiming comprehensive coverage from file counts. Planned commands are not executed observations.

With no consequential finding, report the inspected scope, important checked premises and limitations. Do not fabricate objections or mark untouched rows passed. If quantities help, define their denominator and count only actual checks; repeated readings of one origin are not independently verified observations.

## Findings and severity

For each material finding give identity/location, original passage or observed result, triggering condition, failed contract/inference, impact, evidence status, smallest useful correction and a resolving check where needed. Preserve inference versus observation. Distinguish a demonstrated technical defect, an uncertain empirical design concern and a value/style disagreement.

| Result | Meaning | Disposition |
|---|---|---|
| Supported defect | Concrete evidence or proof shows a bounded error | Repair when authorized; mark the affected step blocked only when required behavior or unacceptable consequence warrants it |
| Unresolved risk | Consequential concern with an actual basis but missing decisive evidence | Preserve uncertainty, effect on the dependent result and next observation; uncertainty alone neither proves nor dismisses it |
| No defect in inspected scope | Actual checks did not establish a consequential issue | Report coverage/limits and avoid broader certification |

Severity follows consequences and required acceptance; evidence confidence is separate. A warning can be blocking and a confirmed minor error nonblocking. A glyph, numerical confidence or aggregate score does not decide that relationship.

## Example report anatomy

```markdown
# {Artifact} review

Object/version: {actual identity}
Goal/scope: {requested behavior and examined material}
Actual mode/work: {direct review or real independent contribution; executed checks}

## Findings
- {ID}: {location and exact passage/result}
  - Trigger/failed condition: ...
  - Consequence and evidence status: ...
  - Correction or resolving check: ...

## Supported and unresolved conclusions
{What changes, what survives independently, missing evidence and coverage limits}

## Actual edits and next allowed action
{Applied/proposed/failed/unperformed separately, with repair verification if performed}
```

Adapt detail to the task; omit empty boilerplate. An optional principle table records only applicable examined checks and unresolved items. Report the actual operation status, not merely the intended next stage.

## Review-to-recovery and implementation handoff

Transfer the exact claim/ID and version, affected scope, accepted objection, reason, evidence and dependent versus independent conclusions. Missing claim identity is not repaired by falling back to an unrelated topic. Recovery applies to a substantively accepted objection, not every excluded candidate or a zero-finding review. A proposed narrowed value cannot erase the original defect.

If the task includes fixes, implement bounded evidence-based corrections and verify them. Review-only returns the findings and possible next steps without editing. A report can support a specification or plan, but neither is a completed implementation. The task's existing authority governs subsequent work; a report creates no approval process or additional permission.

## Authorized storage and textual identity

Save only to the task's requested or established issue/review location. The [record_finding helper](finding-record-tool.md) requires an explicit backlog path and supports a single writer, controlled append and textual duplicate suppression. It does not update an existing entry or detect semantic duplicates; a changed fix hint alone is skipped. If the task requires updating evidence, severity or repair status, inspect and edit the existing entry within scope instead of treating a skipped append as an update.

For manual reconciliation compare object/version, claim/location, trigger and mechanism, retaining independently evidenced concerns and useful chronology. A saved issue, assigned owner and completed repair are different states. Read the actual record after writing. On storage failure deliver the finding and its usable content plus the actual failure; do not label it saved or change it to “no finding.”

## Verify the original failure mechanism

When safe and available, record the old version's actual response to the counterexample. Otherwise label the baseline derived/unavailable instead of inventing a run. Check the repaired triggering input and relevant normal behavior; capture actual values, exit status or rendered result as appropriate.

An added assertion or edited sentence is not verification. The fix must produce the required result: correct normal output may succeed, while a contract requiring rejection needs actual rejection. Silence, error text or exit code alone does not prove the intended behavior. Inspect affected consumers, definitions, source data and links so the remedy does not move the error. Preserve unresolved concerns and partial failures after independent repairs.

